### What is Tabulation?

Tabulation is the bottom-up approach to dynamic programming. Instead of recursing from the goal down to base cases and caching results on the way back up, we start from the base cases and fill a table iteratively, choosing a fill order where every subproblem's dependencies are already solved.

At National Bank of India, Rajesh computes daily running balances starting from the opening balance, adding each day's net transactions in order.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class RunningBalance {
    public static BigDecimal[] computeBalances(BigDecimal opening, BigDecimal[] dailyNet) {
        int days = dailyNet.length;
        BigDecimal[] balance = new BigDecimal[days + 1];
        balance[0] = opening;  // base case

        for (int d = 1; d <= days; d++) {
            balance[d] = balance[d - 1].add(dailyNet[d - 1]);  // fill forward
        }
        return balance;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal opening = new BigDecimal("100000");
        BigDecimal[] daily = {
            new BigDecimal("5000"), new BigDecimal("-2000"),
            new BigDecimal("10000"), new BigDecimal("-3000")
        };
        BigDecimal[] balances = computeBalances(opening, daily);
        for (int i = 0; i < balances.length; i++) {
            System.out.println("Day " + i + ": " + balances[i]);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Day 0: 100000
Day 1: 105000
Day 2: 103000
Day 3: 113000
Day 4: 110000
```

### How Does Tabulation Differ from Memoization?

Memoization is top-down: you write the natural recursion from the goal and cache results. Tabulation is bottom-up: you identify base cases and fill forward. Both have the same asymptotic complexity, but they differ in practical aspects.

| Aspect | Memoization (Top-Down) | Tabulation (Bottom-Up) |
|---|---|---|
| Direction | Goal to base cases | Base cases to goal |
| Mechanism | Recursion + cache | Iteration + table |
| Stack usage | O(depth) call stack | No recursion |
| Overflow risk | StackOverflowError for deep problems | None |
| Space optimization | Harder (cache is sparse) | Easy (keep recent rows) |
| State coverage | Only visited states | All states filled |
| Code style | Natural recursive formulation | Requires explicit fill order |

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class FibComparison {
    // Memoization (top-down)
    public static long fibMemo(int n, long[] memo) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        if (memo[n] != 0) return memo[n];
        memo[n] = fibMemo(n - 1, memo) + fibMemo(n - 2, memo);
        return memo[n];
    }

    // Tabulation (bottom-up)
    public static long fibTab(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        long[] dp = new long[n + 1];
        dp[0] = 0; dp[1] = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + dp[i - 2];
        }
        return dp[n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Memo fib(10): " + fibMemo(10, new long[11]));
        System.out.println("Tab  fib(10): " + fibTab(10));
    }
}
```

```text
Memo fib(10): 55
Tab  fib(10): 55
```

### How Do You Choose the Fill Order?

The fill order must ensure that when you compute a cell, all cells it depends on are already filled. For 1D problems this is usually left-to-right. For 2D problems it depends on the recurrence: sometimes row by row, sometimes by diagonal, sometimes by interval length.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class FillOrderExamples {
    // 1D: dp[i] depends on dp[i-1], dp[i-2]
    //   -> fill left to right

    // 2D grid path: dp[i][j] depends on dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]
    //   -> fill top-to-bottom, left-to-right

    // Interval DP: dp[i][j] depends on dp[i][k], dp[k][j] for i < k < j
    //   -> fill by increasing interval length (j - i)

    // Knapsack: dp[i][w] depends on dp[i-1][w], dp[i-1][w-weight]
    //   -> fill row by row, left-to-right
}
```

### What About Space Optimization?

When each cell depends only on a few recent cells, we can replace the full table with rolling variables. For Fibonacci, two variables suffice. For 2D knapsack, one row suffices with the correct sweep direction.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class FibSpaceOptimized {
    public static long fib(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        long prev2 = 0, prev1 = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            long curr = prev1 + prev2;
            prev2 = prev1;
            prev1 = curr;
        }
        return prev1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("fib(50) = " + fib(50));  // O(1) space!
    }
}
```

```text
fib(50) = 12586269025
```

### When Should You Use Tabulation Over Memoization?

Use tabulation when:

- The problem could have very deep recursion that risks StackOverflowError.
- You need space optimization (rolling variables).
- All subproblems must be solved (the state space is dense).

Use memoization when:

- Only a small fraction of the state space is reachable.
- The recursive formulation is more natural and easier to write.
- You want to avoid working out the correct fill order.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TabulationVsMemoization {
    // Tabulation advantages:
    //   No stack overflow, easy space optimization, no overhead per call
    // Memoization advantages:
    //   Skips unreachable states, natural recursive code, easier to write
}
```

### What Are Common Tabulation Patterns?

The most common tabulation patterns are: 1D linear (Fibonacci, climbing stairs), 1D knapsack (coin change, subset sum), 2D grid (unique paths, minimum path sum), 2D string (LCS, edit distance), and interval (burst balloons, matrix chain).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TabulationPatterns {
    // 1D Linear: dp[i] = f(dp[i-1], dp[i-2])
    //   Examples: Fibonacci, Climbing Stairs, House Robber

    // 1D Knapsack: dp[w] = f(dp[w], dp[w-weight])
    //   Examples: Coin Change, Subset Sum, 0/1 Knapsack

    // 2D Grid: dp[i][j] = f(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])
    //   Examples: Unique Paths, Minimum Path Sum

    // 2D String: dp[i][j] = f(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1], dp[i-1][j-1])
    //   Examples: LCS, Edit Distance, Regex Matching

    // Interval: dp[i][j] = f(dp[i][k], dp[k][j]) for all k
    //   Examples: Burst Balloons, Matrix Chain, Palindrome Partition
}
```

### What Are Common Mistakes in Tabulation?

The first mistake is getting the fill order wrong so that a cell is computed before its dependencies are ready. The second is off-by-one errors in array sizing or loop bounds.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TabulationMistakes {
    // WRONG: array too small
    // int[] dp = new int[n];  // needs dp[n], so size should be n+1

    // CORRECT
    // int[] dp = new int[n + 1];

    // WRONG: fill order for interval DP
    // for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    //   for (int j = i+1; j < n; j++)  // dp[i][j] needs dp[i][k] for k > i
    //     -> correct order is by length: for (int len = 2; len <= n; len++)
}
```

### What Are Common Interview Follow-Up Questions?

Interviewers commonly ask:

- Convert a memoized solution to tabulation: identify the base cases and fill order.
- Optimize the space: identify which previous cells each entry depends on.
- When is memoization better? When the state space is sparse.
- Give an example of a problem where tabulation is essential: very deep recursion like Fibonacci of 100,000.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TabulationFollowUp {
    // Memoized fib(100000) would stack overflow
    // Tabulated fib(100000) works fine with O(1) space
    // That is why tabulation matters for large inputs
}
```
